
A System and Method for a Neuromorphic-Orchestrated, Quantum-Secured 6G Communications Network


Abstract

A system and method for a self-managing, quantum-secured sixth-generation (6G) communications network is disclosed. The system comprises a tri-layered architecture featuring a 6G Transport Layer for high-throughput data transmission, a Quantum Security Layer (QSL) for providing information-theoretic security, and a Neuromorphic Cognitive Orchestration Layer (NCOL) for autonomous network management. The QSL utilizes a hybrid of Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) and Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) to secure network communications against both classical and quantum threats. The NCOL, implemented via a distributed network of neuromorphic co-processors running Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs), provides real-time, low-latency, and energy-efficient control over both the transport and security layers. Key inventive steps include a method for neuromorphic management of QKD trusted relays, wherein the NCOL dynamically assesses relay trustworthiness to route quantum keys along the most secure paths, thereby mitigating critical vulnerabilities in prior art. The invention further includes a system for emergent intent-based networking, where the network self-organizes to fulfill high-level service objectives, and a method for predictive self-healing based on microsecond-level spatio-temporal anomaly detection. A quantum-state-aware digital twin, managed by the NCOL, enables high-fidelity simulation and pre-validation of network optimizations, including the probabilistic behavior of the quantum layer, facilitating true network self-evolution.

Background of the Invention


Field of the Invention

This invention pertains to the field of telecommunications, specifically to the architecture, management, and security of next-generation wireless networks. It further relates to the integration of quantum communication protocols and neuromorphic computing systems for autonomous network orchestration, providing a resilient, intelligent, and secure infrastructure for future digital services.

Description of Related Art

The evolution of wireless communication has been characterized by generational leaps in capability. Projections for the sixth generation (6G) of wireless technology envision a network of unprecedented complexity, requiring a new paradigm of autonomous, intelligent, and efficient control.
Concurrently, the rapid advancement of quantum computing poses a catastrophic threat to the security infrastructure that underpins the entire digital economy. A sufficiently powerful quantum computer will render nearly all currently deployed public-key cryptography systems obsolete. This vulnerability is not a distant threat but an immediate one, due to the "harvest now, decrypt later" attack vector, where adversaries actively intercept and store encrypted data today with the intention of decrypting it once a cryptographically relevant quantum computer becomes available.1
In response, two primary security solutions have emerged: Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) and Quantum Key Distribution (QKD). PQC involves new classical algorithms, while QKD offers information-theoretic security based on the laws of quantum mechanics. However, the practical deployment of QKD over wide-area networks is severely hampered by a critical architectural flaw: its reliance on "trusted relays" or "trusted nodes".1 Due to photon loss, QKD links are limited in distance, necessitating intermediate nodes that receive, process, and retransmit the quantum key. These trusted relays possess the full secret key material, representing a fundamental architectural flaw and a single point of failure. The compromise of a single trusted relay can invalidate the security of the entire communication chain, a vulnerability that has been noted by national security authorities and significantly curtails the promise of QKD.2 This represents a long-felt but unsolved need in the industry.
To manage network complexity, Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) have been widely adopted. However, these AI systems are predominantly based on conventional von Neumann architectures (e.g., GPUs, TPUs) that are ill-suited for the real-time control loops of 6G. As shown in Table 1, these systems exhibit high latency and power consumption, making them incapable of responding to network dynamics at the microsecond timescale required for 6G services or detecting subtle, real-time anomalies indicative of sophisticated security breaches.3
The prior art thus presents a set of profound, interdependent challenges. The escalating complexity of 6G networks demands a more powerful form of autonomous management. The looming quantum threat necessitates a new security paradigm, yet the most promising solution, QKD, contains a fundamental vulnerability that has, to date, prevented its widespread adoption. The conventional AI tools used to manage network complexity are too slow, power-hungry, and reactive to effectively control a 6G system or secure a quantum network in real time. What is needed, and what is absent from the prior art, is a unified architecture that holistically solves these interconnected problems—a system that is simultaneously ultra-fast, ultra-secure, and ultra-intelligent by design.
